  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cloudy and unsettled start to this workweek as \nmultiple weak disturbances pass by.\n\nWe remain under WNW flow with another upper level trough clearing \nthe region later tonight, and with it cooler temperatures\nprevail aloft. Add in a passing mid level shortwave as well, \nand there is a risk for isolated to scattered light showers \ntoday, especially from late morning in to this afternoon. The \nrisk for showers diminishes towards sunset. The greatest risk\nwill be towards the higher terrain of western MA.\n\nBreezy west to northwest winds into Tuesday as colder air moves\ninto southern New England.\n\nDrier weather should prevail tonight and Tuesday. Temperatures\nexpected to be closer to normal as well.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cloudy through midweek with more seasonable \ntemperatures.\n\nAnother trough passes over the region Tuesday night into\nWednesday morning, along with an associated surface low to our\nnortheast, continuing the WNW to NW flow expected for the start \nof the week. 850 mb temperatures return to just below 0C for\nmost of the region as well before improving slightly once more\nThursday ahead an approaching strong closed low. 925 mb\ntemperatures generally remain around 4-6C during this midweek\nperiod. These conditions will continue supporting more\nseasonable temperatures with highs in the 50s and lows in the \n30s and 40s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled weather may return late this week.\n\nTowards the second half of next week, guidance has continued to \nindicate possible development of another low pressure\noriginating from just to the south of the region along/just off \nthe mid- Atlantic coast. This looks to be driven by a multitude \nof factors, starting with a trough developing off the Rockies by\nsometime Tuesday night/evening that will track eastward. As it\ndoes, ridging amplifies over the western CONUS and sets up over \nthe Rockies by sometime Wednesday. The trough from the Rockies \nthen may end up phasing with trough energy from the Canadian\nPrairies, which then tracks southeast, deepening into the Ohio\nValley. This solution has started to become the main consensus\neven among the deterministic global guidance. Surface low\npressure develops over the mid- Atlantic, then tracks northeast. \nPast this point midweek though and closer to the surface,\ndetails become more uncertain.\n\nA lot of spread remains this far out regarding its track and\nwhat the potential impacts to southern New England could look\nlike. 00Z GFS and GEFS guidance continues to favor a warmer,\nwetter solution as the low would track moreso directly over the \nregion, with the colder air aloft not coming in until sometime \nSaturday morning. This would keep precip over much of the region\nas mostly rain, with a changeover to gloppy, wet snow possible \ntowards the eastern slopes of the Berkshires and over the higher\nelevations of the Worcester Hills. At this time, this is not \nexpected to be greatly impactful if this scenario is what pans \nout. Other guidance (ECMWF, Canadian suites) indicates a decent \nbit of colder air aloft, notably 850 mb temperatures below 0C, \napproaching the region a bit earlier, but the center of the low \ntracks closer to the 40N/70W benchmark, which would keep the \nregion drier. Given the multiple facets associated with this \npotential system, more should become clearer as the details \ndeveloping earlier this week come into focus.\n\nOnce this potential system pulls away, we're left with cyclonic\nflow aloft, leading to cloudy skies and cooler temperatures\n(especially as 850 mb temperatures look to fall to around -4C). \nBreezy NW winds look to take hold as well as more northerly to \nNW flow sets up over the region.",
